    def longestCommonPrefix(self, strs: List[str]) -> str:
        if not strs:
            return ""
        shortest = min(strs, key=len)
        for i, char in enumerate(shortest):
            for other in strs:
                if other[i] != char:
                    return shortest[:i]
        return shortest

# The time complexity of this solution is O(S), where S is the sum of all characters in all strings. The space complexity is O(1), since no extra space or data structure is used.
# The idea is to find the smallest string in the list of strings, as having a larger prefix will not help. The loop iterates over the chars of the smallest string. Then for each char in the smallest string, it checks if all strings in the list have the same char in the same index. If yes, then we add it to the result; otherwise we return curr sub-str and the loop ends. In the end, it's possible either longest common prefix is the shortest string or it's substring of it, so return shortest[:i] to check and return the correct answer. If no common prefix, it returns an empty string.

# This approach is useful when dealing with large number of strings. In such cases, looking for the smallest string could save a lot of time because the prefix can never be longer than the smallest string. By comparing the chars of the smallest string with all other strings, the goal is to find the longest prefix.

# It is worth mentioning it can be very helpful when we already know that there is at least one common prefix, so our approach will return immediately. In most other cases, where prefixes can be completely arbitrary, and we don't know anything about the common prefix, this will always check all possible prefixes.

# This approach is also useful when we want to find a longest common suffix in a collection of strings, only that we have to find it in the string reversed i.e find longest common suffix in reversed--------------------